# How many must be immune to stop a disease?
subtitle: "Why a disease twice as contagious needs only a little more immunity, and why measles needs 95 % of children vaccinated"
tags: ["epidemiology herd immunity","disease transmission r0"]
teaches: ["concepts/herd-immunity-threshold","concepts/basic-reproduction-number","methods/vaccination-strategy-planning"]
thumb: "card red"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Kore | a firm, clear epidemiologist; calm and plain, never preachy","take":"lines"}

## How many must be immune to stop a disease?
sources:
    - id: cdc-mmr
      title: 'About measles: vaccine effectiveness'
      authors: US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
      year: 2024
      finding: Two doses of MMR vaccine are about 97 % effective at preventing measles; one dose about 93 %.
    - id: who-measles
      title: Measles fact sheet
      authors: World Health Organization
      year: 2024
      finding: Coverage of 95 % or more with two doses of measles-containing vaccine is needed to prevent outbreaks.

    ```python herd cites ["cdc-mmr","who-measles"] y 405 width 880 controls ["r0","efficacy","coverage"] pane controls hidden true
    import numpy as np
    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
    r0 = Slider(1.2, 18.0, step=0.2, default=2.0, label="R0")
    efficacy = Slider(0.5, 1.0, step=0.01, default=0.9, label="Vaccine efficacy")
    coverage = Slider(0.0, 1.0, step=0.05, default=0.6, label="Coverage")
    plt.figure(figsize=(9, 5.2))
    r = np.linspace(1.05, 18, 300)
    need = 1 - 1 / r0
    today = coverage * efficacy
    plt.plot(r, 1 - 1 / r, color="#b5482e", linewidth=2.5)
    plt.scatter([r0], [need], s=90, zorder=3, color="#b5482e")
    plt.axhline(today, color="gray", linestyle="--", linewidth=2)
    plt.text(17.8, 0.985, "needed", ha="right", color="#b5482e", fontsize=24)
    plt.text(17.8, today - 0.09, "immune today", ha="right", color="gray", fontsize=24)
    plt.xticks([2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18], fontsize=18); plt.yticks([0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1], ["0", "25 %", "50 %", "75 %", "100 %"], fontsize=18)
    plt.xlabel("R0", fontsize=22); plt.ylim(0, 1.05)
    plt.title("needs " + str(round(100 * need)) + " %    today " + str(round(100 * today)) + " %", fontsize=34)
    print("R0", r0, "needs", round(100 * need), "%; today", round(100 * today), "%")
    ```

    card title "How many must be immune to stop a disease?"

Twice as contagious: does a disease then need twice as many people vaccinated before it stops?
    draw herd herd_ctl_r0 parallel true

The first knob is R0: how many people one case infects when nobody is immune.
    highlight herd_ctl_r0

To stop spreading, each case must infect fewer than one. With a share p immune, a case infects R0 times one minus p.
    math thr tex "R_0 \\times (1 - p) < 1" size 56 colors.p #2f6b8f colors.R_0 #b5482e x 500 y 520

Solve for p: the immune share must pass one minus one over R0. That is the herd immunity threshold.
    morph thr tex "p > 1 - \\frac{1}{R_0}" duration 1.5

The other two knobs belong to the vaccination programme: how well the vaccine protects, and how many people get it.
    draw herd_ctl_efficacy herd_ctl_coverage

    erase thr

The curve is that threshold for every R0.
    draw herd_out

At an R0 of two, the dot says half of us must be immune.
    highlight herd_out

The dashed line is who is immune today: sixty percent vaccinated, nine in ten of them protected, so 54 percent. Just past the dot.
    highlight herd_out

Walk R0 up. The share needed leaps from under forty percent to two thirds by an R0 of three, then only crawls: three quarters, four fifths.
    run code herd values.r0.from 1.6 values.r0.to 5.8 values.r0.steps 4

So a disease twice as contagious does not need twice the coverage. From an R0 of two to four, the need goes from a half to three quarters.
    run code herd values.r0 [2,4]

Now measles, whose R0 is usually put at twelve to eighteen. At twelve, ninety-two percent must be immune.
    run code herd values.r0 12

Here is the catch. With a vaccine that protects nine in ten, even vaccinating everyone leaves us short: past an R0 of ten, it can never be enough.
    run code herd values.r0 12 values.efficacy 0.9 values.coverage 1

Two doses of measles vaccine protect about ninety-seven percent. Given to ninety-five percent of children, that clears an R0 of twelve, though not quite eighteen.
    run code herd values.efficacy 0.97 values.coverage 0.95 values.r0 12

That is why the World Health Organization asks for at least ninety-five percent coverage against measles, and why a small dip lets outbreaks back in.
    highlight herd_ctl_coverage

Try it: pick an R0, then find the lowest coverage that clears the dot, first with a weak vaccine, then a strong one.
    explore code herd

    run code herd values.r0 4 values.efficacy 0.9 values.coverage 0.6 every 0.3

    quiz "R0 doubles from 4 to 8. The share that must be immune…"
        right: From three quarters to seven eighths: one minus one over R0 flattens, so twice as contagious needs only a little more, though that little may be beyond a vaccine's reach.
        * Doubles
        + Rises from 75 % to about 88 %
        * Stays the same

